## FSB Directory Scrape

```{r}
# department labels + their faculty/staff directory URLs
dept_urls <- c(
  "Accountancy"                     = "https://miamioh.edu/fsb/directory/?up=/query/all/all/Accountancy/all",
  "Economics"                       = "https://miamioh.edu/fsb/directory/?up=/query/all/all/Economics/all",
  "Entrepreneurship"                = "https://miamioh.edu/fsb/directory/?up=/query/all/all/Entrepreneurship/all",
  "Finance"                         = "https://miamioh.edu/fsb/directory/?up=/query/all/all/Finance/all",
  "Information Systems & Analytics" = "https://miamioh.edu/fsb/directory/?up=/query/all/all/Information%20Systems%20--%20Analytics/all",
  "Marketing"                       = "https://miamioh.edu/fsb/directory/?up=/query/all/all/Marketing/all",
  "Management"                      = "https://miamioh.edu/fsb/directory/?up=/query/all/all/Management/all"
)

# scrapes one department's directory page
scrape_fsb_dept <- function(dept_url) {

  page <- rvest::read_html(dept_url)
  rows <- page |> rvest::html_elements("table tr")

  department <- rows |> rvest::html_element("td:nth-child(1)") |> rvest::html_text2()
  name       <- rows |> rvest::html_element("td strong a")     |> rvest::html_text2()
  website    <- rows |> rvest::html_element("td strong a")     |> rvest::html_attr("href")
  position   <- rows |> rvest::html_element("td i")            |> rvest::html_text2()

  data.frame(department, name, position, website)
}

fsb_directory_df <- purrr::map_df(.x = dept_urls, .f = scrape_fsb_dept)
nrow(fsb_directory_df)
head(fsb_directory_df)

# drop the header row AND keep only the 7 target departments
fsb_directory_df <- fsb_directory_df[
  !is.na(fsb_directory_df$name) & fsb_directory_df$department %in% names(dept_urls),
]

head(fsb_directory_df)
table(fsb_directory_df$department)
```
## AI Assistance

I used ChatISA for parts of this assignment that went past what we covered in class.

- AI helped with a named vector to pair each URL with its department, then using `names()` to get the labels back out. I could have done this with two plain vectors instead, like we did in class, since the department name actually comes from the table itself anyway.
- AI helped with `html_element()` (singular) instead of `html_elements()` (plural) so each row's name, position, and website stayed matched up correctly. I could have avoided this by just dropping the header row first and using the plural version from class.
- AI helped with `td:nth-child(1)` to grab the department column, since it had no class or ID to select by. This one I couldn't really avoid.
- AI helped with `html_attr("href")` to pull the actual link out of the HTML instead of the visible text. `html_text2()` can't do that, so this one I also couldn't avoid.
- AI helped with `is.na()`, `%in%`, and row subsetting to drop a leftover header row and filter out a few staff who weren't in one of the 7 required departments.

Everything else, like `read_html()`, `html_elements()`, `html_text2()`, writing a function, and using `map_df()` across multiple URLs, follows what we did in class.